<h2>The prosecution&rsquo;s chain</h2>
<p>FIR No. 98/2026, PS R.K. Puram, began with the co-accused&rsquo;s arrest on 13 May 2026 and her disclosure naming three suppliers. The applicant was picked up on 6 June 2026; the chargesheet followed on 7 July 2026 under Section 21 NDPS Act. His bail application before the Special Judge (NDPS), Patiala House was dismissed on 24 July 2026, bringing him to the High Court under Section 483 BNSS.</p>

<h2>Quantity first: the gateway question in every NDPS bail</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Quantity (heroin)</th><th>Classification</th><th>Bail consequence</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Up to 5 g</td><td>Small</td><td>Lenient regime; offence punishable up to 1 year under Section 21(a)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>5 g – 250 g</td><td>Intermediate</td><td>Up to 10 years under Section 21(b); Section 37 twin conditions do not apply — ordinary bail principles govern</td></tr>
  <tr><td>250 g and above</td><td>Commercial</td><td>Section 37 bar: bail only on reasonable grounds to believe innocence and unlikelihood of reoffending</td></tr>
</table>
<p>At 7 grams — &ldquo;marginally above the small quantity&rdquo;, as the defence put it — the case sat at the lower end of the intermediate band. The Court expressly recorded that nothing justified importing Section 37&rsquo;s strict rigours.</p>

<h2>The procedural cracks</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The Section 50 notice.</strong> The notice was typed; the reply recorded on it was handwritten — by the IO, who explained the accused was &ldquo;not very literate&rdquo;. Yet the notice bore the accused&rsquo;s signatures in English. The contradiction raised a serious doubt about the document&rsquo;s genuineness.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The search memo.</strong> The prosecution alleged recovery from the applicant&rsquo;s jeans pocket, but the personal search memo reflected no contraband — a gap going to the heart of the recovery.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No independent witness, no video.</strong> No public witness joined the recovery; no videography was done. The State called video recording directory, but the Court flagged the absence of independent witnesses as a relevant factor at the bail stage.</div>
</div>

<h2>The bail calculus</h2>
<p>Quoting the principles summarised in <em>Anil Mahajan</em> — personal liberty as a constitutional value, bail as the rule, the object of bail being attendance at trial rather than pre-conviction punishment, and the two paramount considerations of flight risk and tampering — the Court weighed: a 25-year-old with no criminal antecedents; custody since 6 June 2026 with satisfactory jail conduct per the nominal roll; completed investigation; and a trial that had not even reached charge. Continued incarceration, it held, would serve no useful purpose. Bail was granted on a Rs 25,000 personal bond with one surety, residence and mobile-number conditions, and the usual embargo on inducement or tampering, with the IO directed to monitor complaints.</p>

<h2>Takeaways for practice</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Attack the paperwork, not just the quantity.</strong> In intermediate-quantity cases, documentary inconsistencies — notice, search memo, seizure memo — convert a routine application into a strong one.</p>
  <p><strong>Parity within the array matters in NDPS too.</strong> The alleged supplier&rsquo;s anticipatory bail made the applicant&rsquo;s continued custody harder to justify.</p>
  <p><strong>Nominal rolls are underused.</strong> A clean jail-conduct record is inexpensive, official corroboration of the no-risk argument.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises a judgment of the Delhi High Court for general information. It is not legal advice; NDPS bail outcomes depend on quantity, recovery evidence and antecedents in each case.</div>
